    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "There are 13 possibilities for choosing the first card to be a 6. Once that card has been chosen, there will be only 4 possibilities left for the second card, a Queen, since there are only 4 Queens left in the deck.\nIn total, there are 13 * 4 = 52 ways to satisfy the condition of the problem.\nThe total number of ways to choose any 2 cards from the deck is 52, 51 = 2652, since the order in which we choose the first and second card doesn't matter.\nTherefore, the probability of getting 1st card being a 6 and 2nd card being a Queen is 52 out of 2652, or 52/2652 = 1/51.\nThe answer is: 1/51",
