    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can break it down into two steps:\n\n1. Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 as the first card.\n2. Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen as the second card, given that a 6 has already been drawn.\n\nThere are 4 ⋅ 52 cards total in the deck, out of which there are 4 sixes and 4 queens. \nThe probability of drawing a six as the first card is therefore 4/52 or 1/13.\nAfter this, there are 51 cards left in the deck, out of which there are still 4 queens. The probability of drawing a queen as the second card is therefore 4/51.\nThus, the probability of both of these occurring is (1/13) * (4/51) = (4/663).",
