    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $u$ be the event that the first card is a 6 and let $v$ be the event that the second card is a queen.  Then $\\mathbf{P}(u)= \\dfrac{1}{52}$ and $\\mathbf{P}(v)= \\dfrac{4}{52}= \\dfrac{1}{13}$.  Also, the cards being dealt are independent events.  By the multiplication rule for independent events, we find that \\[\\mathbf{P}(u\\cap v)= \\mathbf{P}(u)\\times\\mathbf{P}(v)= \\dfrac{1}{52}\\times \\dfrac{1}{13}= \\dfrac{1}{676}.\\]",
